    essay5Desc: "A page dedicated to the memory of the founder's brother.",
    founderBio: "Innocent Forteh is a Cameroon-based servant leadership teacher with more than twenty years of professional service across U.S. Government and international development institutions, having stewarded programmes worth more than US$160 million across Africa. He has been recognised among top performers for over a decade.\n\nMore importantly, he is a husband to Linda and a disciple of Jesus. He is a long-time student of biblical leadership, and his writing has appeared, quietly, on multiple blogs — where leadership, faith, family and grief are written about in the same voice. Those archives have shaped a generation of leaders.\n\nThis school carries two names. The first is Ngochi — the family name of his younger brother Derick, who confessed Jesus as Lord on his deathbed and passed into eternity on the night of Thursday, May 7, 2020, after a brief illness. The pain was great and the loss real; the family entered what Innocent later named the crucible. Out of that crucible came the discipline of writing publicly about grief, about the unchanging faithfulness of God when prayers are answered differently than asked, and about the wilderlove that follows. This academy is dedicated to Derick Ngochi's memory and to every leader who, like him, will one day pass the baton.\n\nThe second name is Ndezo — his grandmother, who shined like Derick in her service and love for people. Together: Ngochi & Ndezo. The man who has gone before, and the grandmother who modelled service, continue to show the way.\n\nInnocent founded Team21 as a free voluntary servant leadership programme. This School of Champions is the formalised, deepened, certified version of that original work — built so that the teaching can outlast the teacher.\n\nHis convictions are simple and uncompromising. The great African renewal is being held back not by lack of resources but by lack of leaders who refuse the corruption of self. Africa's stagnation is not the fault of Christianity, or pastors, or prayer — it is the fault of poor leadership in every sphere: home, street, palace. The church has too often discipled people into religion without forming them into servants. The best test of any leadership is whether those served grow into servants themselves. And every leader, sooner or later, must walk through the crucible — not around it. From that crucible comes the only kind of leader worth raising.",
